### 2.5 Hash Table Insert, Lookup, and Pop (Slide 12)

**Code:**
```c
void insert(string key, void *binding) {
    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;
    table[index] = Bucket(key, binding, table[index]);
}

void *lookup(string key) {
    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;
    struct bucket *b;
    for (b = table[index]; b; b = b->next)
        if (0 == strcmp(b->key, key))
            return b->binding;
    return NULL;
}

void pop(string key) {
    int index = hash(key) % SIZE;
    table[index] = table[index]->next;
}
```

**Explanation:**
- **`insert()`**: Computes the bucket index, then prepends a new bucket at the **head** of the chain. This effectively **shadows** any existing binding for the same key because lookups find the head-most bucket first. O(1) average case.
- **`lookup()`**: Computes the bucket index, then traverses the collision chain linearly, comparing keys with `strcmp()`. Returns the binding of the first matching bucket (the most recent, innermost one). O(1) average case.
- **`pop()`**: Simply advances the head of the chain (`table[index] = table[index]->next`), effectively removing the most recently inserted binding. This works **only if** the key being popped is the same as the one at the head — which is guaranteed because insert puts the new binding at the head.

