```
When inserting a ↦ τ2 where σ already contains a ↦ τ1:

Before:  hash(a) -> <a, τ1>
After:   hash(a) -> <a, τ2> -> <a, τ1>

After pop(a):  hash(a) -> <a, τ1>
```

**Explanation:**
When a new binding for an existing key is inserted, it is placed at the **head of the chain**, shadowing the older binding. The older binding `<a, τ1>` remains in the chain but is "hidden" behind `<a, τ2>`. When the scope ends, `pop(a)` removes the head bucket, revealing `<a, τ1>` again. This gives the correct stack-like, FIFO behavior for scope management — insertions and pops work in a **stack-like fashion**.

