### 2.10 Symbol Implementation — The Symbol Module (Slide 22)

**Code:**
```c
static S_symbol mksymbol(string name, S_symbol next) {
    S_symbol s = checked_malloc(sizeof(*s));
    s->name = name;
    s->next = next;
    return s;
}

S_symbol S_symbol(string name) {
    int index = hash(name) % SIZE;
    S_symbol syms = hashtable[index], sym;
    for (sym = syms; sym; sym = sym->next)
        if (0 == strcmp(sym->name, name))
            return sym;
    sym = mksymbol(name, syms);
    hashtable[index] = sym;
    return sym;
}

string S_name(S_symbol sym) {
    return sym->name;
}
```

**Explanation:**
- **`mksymbol()`**: Creates a new symbol node containing the name string and a pointer to the next symbol in the chain.
- **`S_symbol(name)`**: This implements **symbol interning**. It first checks the hash table bucket for an existing symbol with the same name. If found, it returns the existing symbol (guaranteeing uniqueness). If not found, it creates a new symbol and inserts it at the head of the chain.
- **`S_name()`**: Simply returns the string stored in the symbol. This is the only way to get the string back out.
- **Benefits**: Once a string is converted to a symbol, all subsequent comparisons are pointer/integer comparisons (O(1)) rather than character-by-character string comparisons. The integer hash key can also be the symbol pointer itself.

